From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: mt6797: claim the audio PLL mux group, or the boot wedges

THIS IS THE FIX FOR #85. It is a boot-reliability fix that happens to live in
the audio driver.

MT6797 loses roughly half its reboots to a 5-20 minute loop. A failing boot,
captured through the ramoops console zone (which survives this SoC's reset, so
a dead boot is readable on the next live one), dies here:

  [    1.010716] clk-unused: gating mm_dsi1_interface_clock
  [    1.011373] clk-unused: gating mm_dsi0_interface_clock
  [    1.012031] clk-unused: gating audio_h_sel
  [    1.012557] clk-unused: gating aud_2_sel
  [    1.013062] clk-unused: gating aud_1_sel
  [   24.025716] watchdog: Watchdog detected hard LOCKUP on cpu 3

Five clocks of 111. A healthy boot prints its next line one millisecond after
"clk: Disabling unused clocks"; this one printed nothing for twenty-three
seconds and then lost two CPUs. Gate a clock a block silently depends on and the
next MMIO to it never returns -- a hard lockup on whichever CPU touches it,
which is why the locked pair varies between failures. /init never runs, systemd
never claims /dev/watchdog, and watchdog.open_timeout=120 reclaims the machine:
measured at 119, 120, 119, 120 and 118 seconds across five failures.

WHY THEY WERE GATED: NOBODY ASKED FOR THEM.

  aud_1_sel   = CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1     (clk-mt6797.c:386)
  aud_2_sel   = CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_2     (clk-mt6797.c:388)
  audio_h_sel = CLK_TOP_MUX_AUDIO_H   (clk-mt6797.c:396)

Grepping every device tree in the tree for those IDs returns mt8183 -- and
nothing else. mt8183's audio node claims exactly this group, alongside
APLL1_CK/APLL2_CK, and so does mt7622's. MT6797's claimed none of them, and
mt6797-afe-clk.c asks for "top_mux_audio" and "top_mux_aud_intbus", which are
CLK_TOP_MUX_AUDIO and CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_INTBUS -- different muxes. So the audio
PLL path had enable_count 0 and the sweep took it.

WHY IT WAS INTERMITTENT. The AFE takes all its clocks with devm_clk_get() -- get
only, no enable -- and enables four of them from mt6797_afe_runtime_resume().
Its probe defers ("snd_soc_register_card fail -517" appears in every boot log),
so whether it is runtime-resumed when clk_disable_unused() runs varies from boot
to boot. A clock a driver merely *gets* is still a clock the sweep will gate.

This is the same defect, in the same shape, as the one closed on 2026-07-06:
8250_mtk never enabled the UART's own baud clock, the sweep gated it, and the
boot went silent at exactly this line. That was repaired with
devm_clk_get_enabled(). Same repair here, for the three that nothing else holds.

AND IT EXPLAINS WHY netconsole= ON THE COMMAND LINE "FIXED" THE LOOP. Measured:
#110 with it, 24/24 clean reboot cycles; #111, the same tree with only that
parameter deleted, looped on its first reboot. netpoll_setup() runs at
late_initcall -- one level before late_initcall_sync -- and dev_open()s usb0,
which both takes clock references and delays the sweep. It was never a cure. It
moved a race, and it has been load-bearing ever since.

MEASURED: build #115 (netconsole removed, clk_ignore_unused added) gave 12/12
clean cycles where #113 looped on its first reboot, which is what established
the sweep as the mechanism before this fix existed.

Note mm_dsi0/1_interface_clock are gated immediately before, in the same
unclaimed position. They are not addressed here and are worth the same look.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -1841,6 +1841,14 @@
 		reg = <0 0x11220000 0 0x10000>;
 		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 151 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
 		power-domains = <&scpsys MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_AUDIO>;
+		/*
+		 * #85: aud_1_sel / aud_2_sel / audio_h_sel (CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1,
+		 * _AUD_2, _AUDIO_H) were claimed by NOTHING in this tree, so
+		 * clk_disable_unused() gated them and the boot wedged -- a
+		 * captured failing boot dies at exactly "gating aud_1_sel".
+		 * mt8183 and mt7622 both claim this group from their audio
+		 * nodes; MT6797 did not. See 04-docs/captures/85-boot-loop-20260828/.
+		 */
 		clocks = <&infrasys CLK_INFRA_AUDIO>,
 			 <&infrasys CLK_INFRA_AUDIO_26M>,
 			 <&infrasys CLK_INFRA_AUDIO_26M_PAD_TOP>,
@@ -1848,7 +1856,10 @@
 			 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_INTBUS>,
 			 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_SYSPLL3_D4>,
 			 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_SYSPLL1_D4>,
-			 <&clk26m>;
+			 <&clk26m>,
+			 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1>,
+			 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_2>,
+			 <&topckgen CLK_TOP_MUX_AUDIO_H>;
 		clock-names = "infra_sys_audio_clk",
 			      "infra_sys_audio_26m",
 			      "mtkaif_26m_clk",
@@ -1856,7 +1867,10 @@
 			      "top_mux_aud_intbus",
 			      "top_sys_pll3_d4",
 			      "top_sys_pll1_d4",
-			      "top_clk26m_clk";
+			      "top_clk26m_clk",
+			      "top_mux_aud_1",
+			      "top_mux_aud_2",
+			      "top_mux_audio_h";
 	};
 
 	sound {
--- a/sound/soc/mediatek/mt6797/mt6797-afe-clk.c
+++ b/sound/soc/mediatek/mt6797/mt6797-afe-clk.c
@@ -18,6 +18,9 @@
 	CLK_TOP_SYSPLL3_D4,
 	CLK_TOP_SYSPLL1_D4,
 	CLK_CLK26M,
+	CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1,	/* #85 -- see aud_clks[] */
+	CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_2,
+	CLK_TOP_MUX_AUDIO_H,
 	CLK_NUM
 };
 
@@ -29,6 +32,21 @@
 	[CLK_TOP_SYSPLL3_D4] = "top_sys_pll3_d4",
 	[CLK_TOP_SYSPLL1_D4] = "top_sys_pll1_d4",
 	[CLK_CLK26M] = "top_clk26m_clk",
+	/*
+	 * #85. These three were requested by nothing at all, so
+	 * clk_disable_unused() gated them at late_initcall_sync and the boot
+	 * wedged -- a captured failing boot dies at exactly
+	 * "clk-unused: gating aud_1_sel". mt8183 and mt7622 both claim this
+	 * group from their audio nodes.
+	 *
+	 * They are taken with devm_clk_get_enabled() below rather than plain
+	 * devm_clk_get(), which is the same fix 8250_mtk needed on 2026-07-06
+	 * for the UART's baud clock and for the identical symptom. A clock this
+	 * driver merely *gets* is still a clock the sweep will gate.
+	 */
+	[CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1] = "top_mux_aud_1",
+	[CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_2] = "top_mux_aud_2",
+	[CLK_TOP_MUX_AUDIO_H] = "top_mux_audio_h",
 };
 
 int mt6797_init_clock(struct mtk_base_afe *afe)
@@ -42,7 +60,17 @@
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < CLK_NUM; i++) {
-		afe_priv->clk[i] = devm_clk_get(afe->dev, aud_clks[i]);
+		/*
+		 * #85: the last three are taken ENABLED and never released, so
+		 * clk_disable_unused() cannot gate them. The rest keep the
+		 * original get-only behaviour; this driver enables those it
+		 * needs from runtime_resume.
+		 */
+		if (i >= CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1)
+			afe_priv->clk[i] = devm_clk_get_enabled(afe->dev,
+							        aud_clks[i]);
+		else
+			afe_priv->clk[i] = devm_clk_get(afe->dev, aud_clks[i]);
 		if (IS_ERR(afe_priv->clk[i])) {
 			dev_err(afe->dev, "%s(), devm_clk_get %s fail, ret %ld\n",
 				__func__, aud_clks[i],
